{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 10, :comments ["<b>You shall not enter his house to take his pledge.</b> To take his pledge — neither the lender nor the agent of the court. Now this is a quality of compassion: the Torah has pity on the borrower's shame when others search through his household vessels, and further, for the reason that they not come to quarrel and bloodshed. But a guarantor — it is permitted to enter his house to take his pledge; that is what Solomon, peace be upon him, said (Proverbs 20:16): ״לקח בגדו כי ערב זר״. And it would have been more fitting to take pledge from the borrower, who ate and drank and squandered the money, more than from the guarantor, into whose hands the money never came. But the reason for this is that the borrower borrowed only under duress, whereas the guarantor entered as guarantor out of abundance; therefore the Torah permitted taking pledge from the guarantor and not from the borrower."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 24, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:50:48.564145376Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}